---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { tiers, units, specialisations, readinessModules, programme } from "../data/programme.js";
import data from "../data/scenarios.json";

const nFor = (uid) => data.scenarios.filter((s) => s.unit === uid).length;
const hFor = (uid) =>
  Math.round(
    data.scenarios
      .filter((s) => s.unit === uid)
      .reduce((a, s) => a + ({ micro: 0.6, build: 2, ship: 7 }[s.size] || 0), 0)
  );
const t3Hours = readinessModules.reduce((a, m) => a + m.hours, 0);
---
<Base
  title="Programme structure"
  description="Three tiers: a self-paced 440-hour core covering every module with 102 mandatory scenarios, six specialisation tracks, and an instructor-led Deployment Readiness programme with a live project and a supervisor reference."
  wide={true}
>
  <h1>Programme structure</h1>
  <p class="muted" style="max-width:84ch;font-size:1.05rem">
    Three tiers with three different delivery models. Tiers 1 and 2 are self-paced and machine-graded, and run
    entirely on your own laptop. Tier 3 is cohort-based with real trainers — and is the only tier that can
    produce the thing a self-paced course never can: a named professional who will vouch for you.
  </p>

  <div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);margin-top:26px">
    {tiers.map((t) => (
      <div class={`card tierc ${t.accent}`}>
        <span class="pill d">{t.n}</span>
        <h2 style="margin:9px 0 2px;font-size:1.3rem">{t.name}</h2>
        <p style="font-weight:600;font-size:14px;margin:0;color:var(--accent)">{t.tagline}</p>
        <p style="font-size:13.6px;margin:11px 0 0">{t.blurb}</p>
        <div style="margin-top:12px">
          <span class="pill">{t.hours} h{t.hoursNote ? ` ${t.hoursNote}` : ""}</span>
          <span class="pill d">{t.delivery}</span>
          <span class="pill d">{t.grading}</span>
        </div>
        <ul class="tight" style="font-size:13.2px;margin-top:11px">{t.facts.map((f) => <li>{f}</li>)}</ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>Why the core is unusually broad.</strong> Practising one thing deeply does not transfer to the next
    thing — the measured far-transfer effect in the research literature is essentially zero. To be ready for
    several kinds of work you have to have practised several kinds. So the core covers every module, and depth
    is bought afterwards in Tier 2, by choice rather than by default.
  </div>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Tier 1 · Core — the twelve units</h2>
    <p class="muted">Each unit ends with something you can show. Weeks 1–5 install nothing at all — the first five units run in the browser, because that is where dropout concentrates.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:6%">#</th><th style="width:24%">Unit</th><th style="width:16%">Environment</th><th style="width:12%">Scenarios</th><th>Ship artifact</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{units.map((u) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{u.id}</strong></td>
          <td>{u.name}</td>
          <td class="muted">{u.env}</td>
          <td><a href={`/scenarios#browse`} style="text-decoration:none">{nFor(u.id)}</a> <span class="muted">· {hFor(u.id)} h</span></td>
          <td>{u.artifact}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p style="margin-top:14px">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/scenarios">Browse all {programme.scenarioCount} scenarios →</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Tier 2 · Specialisations</h2>
    <p class="muted">Taken after core, in any number. Hardware-heavy material lives here, with free-cloud escape hatches so an 8 GB laptop is never the blocker.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:6%">#</th><th style="width:20%">Track</th><th>Adds</th><th style="width:17%">Environment</th><th style="width:16%">Target</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{specialisations.map((s) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{s.id}</strong></td><td>{s.name}</td><td class="muted">{s.adds}</td><td>{s.env}</td><td>{s.target}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Tier 3 · Deployment Readiness</h2>
    <p class="muted">{t3Hours} hours · 12 weeks full-time or 24 part-time · entry requires Core plus at least one specialisation. Everything here needs a human, which is exactly why it is not in Tiers 1 and 2.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:6%">#</th><th style="width:20%">Module</th><th style="width:7%">Hours</th><th>Covers</th><th style="width:18%">Needs a human for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{readinessModules.map((m) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{m.id}</strong></td>
          <td>{m.doc ? <a href="/ojt" style="text-decoration:none">{m.name}</a> : m.name}</td>
          <td>{m.hours}</td>
          <td class="muted" style="font-size:13px">{m.covers}</td>
          <td>{m.human}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px">
      <strong>Tier 3 also fixes the completion problem.</strong> The same programming course measured both ways,
      across more than 8,000 learners, completed at 45% on a fixed schedule and 13% self-paced. Tiers 1 and 2
      manage that risk through unit shape — many short units, each with its own finish line. Tier 3 manages it
      with a cohort.
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:14px"><a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/ojt">How the live project runs →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>What this programme does not do</h2>
    <div class="warnbox">
      <strong>No government certificate in Tiers 1 and 2.</strong> An unsupervised learner with a frontier model
      in the next tab cannot be credibly certified — in a controlled study inside a live university exam system,
      94% of AI-written submissions went undetected. Tiers 1 and 2 issue evidence, not credentials: a public
      repository, measured results, and artifacts an employer can inspect. Tier 3, which has supervision, cohorts
      and a panel, is the tier where a formal credential would be defensible.
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="font-size:13.5px;margin-top:14px">
      The earlier NSQF-aligned specification is retained for reference at
      <a href="/curriculum">Curriculum</a> and <a href="/compliance">Compliance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
